1 NAME Sarah Ann /Ekelund/
2 GIVN Sarah Ann
2 SURN Ekelund
2 _MARNM Skillman
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 8 MAY 1926
2 PLAC Hibbing
2 ADDR
3 STAE Minnesota
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 2 JUN 2012
2 PLAC Bay City
2 ADDR
3 STAE Michigan
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sara Ekelund Skillman had, in straight descending line, medieval royal Swedish ancestors such as Olof Skotkonung, king of Sweden 995 - 1022.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sara (Sally) Ekelund Skillman (86), passed away June 2, 2012, in her home in Bay City, Michigan.   Sally lived a vibrant and full life surrounded by a loving family right up to her last days.  Born May 8, 1926, in Hibbing, Minneso
2 CONC ta to Dr. Clifford T. (1893-1967) and Katharine Weiser Ekelund (1900-1995), she was the oldest of three daughters.  She graduated from Kingswood School Cranbrook in 1943 and attended Vassar College and Michigan State University, w
2 CONC here she studied English and Modern Languages. In July of 1947 she married Cranbrook graduate and naval officer Newton (Skip) Skillman Jr. in Pontiac, Michigan, and together they shared 55 years raising a family, exploring the wor
2 CONC ld and celebrating life.  They drove away from their wedding in a streamline trailer bound for Colorado, the first of many adventures on the road. Over her lifetime, she travelled to nearly 100 countries.  Sally and Newt first set
2 CONC tled in Farmington, Michigan, where son Christopher Thoren was born in 1949.  Two years later, they purchased and moved into the Senior Skillman home at Lake Angelus and lived there until moving to Bay City in 1972.  During her La
2 CONC ke Angelus years, she raised five children and their multiple pets including championship Old English Sheepdogs, various cats, a long-lived cockatiel and the occasional wild animal rescued by one of her children.  She and Newt hos
2 CONC ted many elaborate (sometimes costumed) parties and organized traveling supper clubs with neighbors.  An annual highlight was the 4th of July picnic, complete with fireworks from the island in the middle of the lake.  Sally was a 
2 CONC member of the Detroit Junior League and co-founder of the Birmingham Junior League.  She was an avid gardener and founded the Lake Angelus branch of the Women’s National Farm and Garden Association.  She served multiple terms on t
2 CONC he Board of the Michigan chapter and chaired their Bicentennial Committee in 1976.  She also served a term on the National Board.  She was a certified judge for flower shows and her garden at Lake Angelus was a haven for her child
2 CONC ren and neighbors with its small ponds, rock-lined brook, flowers, blooming shrubs and shade trees.   Sally was also a member of the Detroit Art Institute and served on the Steering Committee of the Detroit Grand Opera Association
2 CONC  which was responsible for bringing the Metropolitan Opera to Detroit.   That work lead to the creation of the Michigan Opera Theatre, founded in 1971.  Closer to home, Sally served on the Grand Opening Committee of the Pontiac Cr
2 CONC eative Arts Center, founded in 1965 by family friend Dr. Harold Furlong.  Sally was a member of All Saints Episcopal Church of Pontiac and Trinity Episcopal Church in Bay City and served on the Altar Guilds of both for over 50 yea
2 CONC rs.  To help establish the St. Mary’s Church parish in Lake Orion, she organized the first St. Mary’s Church Fair in 1958, which is still running to this day.  She was also instrumental in establishing the Girl’s Friendly Society 
2 CONC camp in Ortonville, a faith-based summer camp for girls.  In Bay City, she joined Trinity Episcopal Church and continued her work on the Altar Guild and as a Lector for Sunday services.  After raising their family, Sally and Newt 
2 CONC opened Tootin’ Turtles Motorhome Rentals and the Tortoise Shell Gift Shop in the early 1970s, matching their love of travel with a love of fine gifts.  In Bay City, Sally served on the Board of Child and Family Services, including
2 CONC  a couple of terms as president.  She also continued her work in the world of gardening, with the Bay City Garden Club, serving on many flower show committees.  In 1975, she became the Promotion Director and Manager of Hampton Squ
2 CONC are Mall, the first enclosed mall in Bay City.  In the early 1980s she worked as a Real Estate Agent in Bay City before becoming certified as a Travel Agent in 1985.  She worked for Opperman Lee Travel in both Bay City and Saginaw
2 CONC  until she retired in 2011.  Sally was an avid reader and treasured the discoveries she made with the James Girls Book Club.  Sally is survived by her two sisters Mary Sue Ekelund Meyer (Weston) and Georgia “Dodie” Ekelund Smith (
2 CONC Bruce); five children Christopher Skillman (Nancy) of Tawas City, Amy  Skillman of Harrisburg, PA, Newton Skillman III (Yvonne) of Clarkston, Stephen Skillman (Pamela) of Bay City and Tim Skillman (Jean) of Los Angeles, CA.; nine 
2 CONC grandchildren Derek Skillman (Katie) of Kasson, MN, Stacey Skillman of Auburn Hills, Jordan Skillman of Pensacola, FL, Spencer and Erica Skillman of Ann Arbor, Philip and Mary Skillman of Clarkston and Tate and Lakin Skillman of L
2 CONC os Angeles; and one great granddaughter Lorelei Skillman of Kasson, MN.   She is predeceased by her husband Newton Skillman Jr. (February 2002).  Visitation will be held at Penzien Steele Funeral Home, 608 N. Madison Ave., Bay Cit
2 CONC y, Michigan, Friday June 8, 5 – 8 pm and Saturday, June 9, 1 – 4 pm.  Funeral services will be held Sunday, June 10, 2012 at 2:00 pm Trinity Episcopal Church 815 N. Grant Street, Bay City.  Memorial Service and Interment will be a
2 CONC t White Chapel Cemetery, Troy on Monday, June 11 at 11 am Reception to follow. In lieu of flowers, the family would love donations to be sent in Sally’s name to Cranbrook School (the Kingswood Restoration & Preservation Fund), Blo
2 CONC omfield Hills, Michigan; Trinity Episcopal Church, McLaren Visiting Nurse and Hospice of Bay City; or, we ask that you plant a tree or flowering shrub in her honor in your town or neighborhood.  Bright, worldly, and ever-gracious,
2 CONC  Sally believed in living life large. 
